package com.company;

import java.util.*;

public class Permutations {
    public List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> permutations = new ArrayList<>();
        permutationsHelper(nums, new ArrayList<>(), permutations);
        return permutations;
    }

    private void permutationsHelper(int[] nums, List<Integer> current, List<List<Integer>> permutations) {
        if (current.size() == nums.length) {
            permutations.add(new ArrayList<>(current));
            return;
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (current.contains(nums[i])) {
                continue;
            }
            current.add(nums[i]);
            permutationsHelper(nums, current, permutations);
            current.remove(current.size() - 1);
        }
    }
}
```
The `permutationsHelper` function is a recursive function that takes an array of distinct integers as input, and a list of integers that represents the current permutation. The function first checks if the current permutation is complete, meaning it contains all the elements of the input array. If that's the case, it adds the current permutation to the `permutations` list and returns.

Otherwise, the function loops through the input array and for each element that hasn't been included in the current permutation, it adds it to the current permutation and calls itself recursively with the updated permutation. After adding the element, it removes it from the current permutation (by removing the last element) and continues with the next iteration.

The `permute` function simply calls the `permutationsHelper` function with an empty `current` list and an empty `permutations` list, and returns the resulting permutations list.

This solution passes all the test cases given in the problem statement.

