public static int countOilDeposits(char[][] grid) {
    int n = grid.length;
    int m = grid[0].length;
    int count = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {
            if (grid[i][j] == '@') {
                count++;
                DFS(grid, i, j, '@');
            }
        }
    }

    return count;
}

private static void DFS(char[][] grid, int i, int j, char color) {
    if (i < 0 || i >= grid.length || j < 0 || j >= grid[0].length || grid[i][j] != color) {
        return;
    }

    grid[i][j] = '#';
    DFS(grid, i + 1, j, color);
    DFS(grid, i - 1, j, color);
    DFS(grid, i, j + 1, color);
    DFS(grid, i, j - 1, color);
}
```
This method first initializes a 2D array `grid` with the dimensions of the input grid, and a variable `count` to keep track of the number of distinct oil deposits.

The nested for loops iterate over the grid and for each cell if it contains the symbol '@', we increase the count by 1, and apply a DFS to fill in all the adjacent cells with the same color.

The DFS function takes the `grid`, the current `i` and `j` indices, and the `color` of the oil deposit as input. The DFS function checks if the current cell meets the following conditions:

* The current cell is within the bounds of the grid.
* The current cell is not already visited / colored.
* The current cell contains the symbol '@'.

If all the conditions are met, we color the current cell with the symbol '#' and apply a DFS to all the adjacent cells recursively.

This ensures that we only count each oil deposit once and cover all the cells in each deposit by visiting and coloring all the adjacent cells.

Finally, the method returns the updated count of oil deposits.

