public static int maxRegions(int N) {
    int regions = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
        int r = 1;
        for (int j = 0; j < N; j++) {
            if (j != i) {
                r *= (i + j);
                r %= 1000000007;
            }
        }
        regions = Math.max(regions, r);
    }
    return regions;
}
```
This solution uses the formula `n \* (n - 1) * (n - 2) * ... * 1` to calculate the number of regions for each triangle. The maximum value of this product is the final answer. The loop starts with `i = 0` and iterates up to `N - 1`. The inner loop starts with `j = 0` and iterates up to `N - 1`. Each iteration of the inner loop calculates the product of the numbers `(i + j)` for `j` ranging from `0` to `N - 1`, excluding `j = i`. The result is then modulo-divided by `1000000007` to prevent overflow. The maximum value of all these products is returned as the final answer.

public static int maxRegions(int N) {
    return (N * (N + 1)) / 2;
}

