Turns out that the problem is a classic one in computer science and there is a well-known algorithm to solve it efficienty called the Connected Components algorithm, which is used to detect clusters of 1s in a binary graph.

The algorithm is explained in detail in the video lecture on the Connected Components, however, the basic idea is to iterate through the matrix and for each cell check if it is connected to any 1s already encountered. If it is connected to a 1 then mark it as part of the cluster, otherwise start a new cluster. The runtime of this algorithm is O(n) where n is the number of elements in the matrix.
```java
public static int longestLine(int[][] mat) {
    // Initialize the output variable
    int maxLen = 0;

    // Iterate through the matrix and find the longest continuous line
    for (int i = 0; i < mat.length; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < mat[0].length; j++) {
            // If the cell is a 1
            if (mat[i][j] == 1) {
                // Mark the cell as visited
                mat[i][j] = -1;
                // Check if the cell is connected to any 1s in the previous row
                if (i > 0 && mat[i-1][j] == 1) {
                    // If connected, mark both cells as part of the cluster
                    mat[i][j] = mat[i-1][j] = -1;
                }
                // Check if the cell is connected to any 1s in the previous column
                if (j > 0 && mat[i][j-1] == 1) {
                    // If connected, mark both cells as part of the cluster
                    mat[i][j] = mat[i][j-1] = -1;
                }
                // Update the maximum length if necessary
                maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, 1 + connectedComponentLength(mat, i, j));
            }
        }
    }

    // Return the maximum length
    return maxLen;
}

private static int connectedComponentLength(int[][] mat, int i, int j) {
    // Initialize the output variable
    int len = 0;

    // Check if the cell is connected to any 1s in the up, down, left, and right directions
    if (i > 0 && mat[i-1][j] == -1) {
        // If connected, mark the cell as part of the cluster
        mat[i-1][j] = -1;
        // Recursively call the function for the previous row and increment the length
        len += connectedComponentLength(mat, i-1, j);
    }
    if (i < mat.length - 1 && mat[i+1][j] == -1) {
        // If connected, mark the cell as part of the cluster
        mat[i+1][j] = -1;
        // Recursively call the function for the next row and increment the length
        len += connectedComponentLength(mat, i+1, j);
    }
    if (j > 0 && mat[i][j-1] == -1) {
        // If connected, mark the cell as part of the cluster
        mat[i][j-1] = -1;
        // Recursively call the function for the previous column and increment the length
        len += connectedComponentLength(mat, i, j-1);
    }
    if (j < mat[0].length - 1 && mat[i][j+1] == -1) {
        // If connected, mark the cell as part of the cluster
        mat[i][j+1] = -1;
        // Recursively call the function for the next column and increment the length
        len += connectedComponentLength(mat, i, j+1);
    }

    // Return the length
    return len;
}
```
The `longestLine` method iterates through the matrix and for each cell checks if it is connected to any 1s in the previous row, previous column, next row, or next column. If it is connected, then the method marks the cell as part of the cluster. The method also updates the maximum length if necessary. The `connectedComponentLength` method is a helper function that recursively calls itself for the neighboring cells and updates the length of the connected component.

